---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Spider Solitaire: 1, 2, or 4 Suits? Which to Play | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Spider Solitaire comes in 1-suit, 2-suit, and 4-suit versions — the number of suits is the difficulty dial. Compare them and find out which one you should play.';
const updated = '2026-06-13';
const heading = 'Spider Solitaire: 1, 2, or 4 Suits?';
const subtitle =
  'The rules never change — only the suit count does. Here is what each version asks of you, and how to pick the right one.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Spider: 1, 2 or 4 Suits', href: '/guides/spider-1-2-4-suits/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is 1, 2, or 4 suit Spider hardest?',
    a: 'Four-suit Spider is by far the hardest. With all four suits in play, only true same-suit runs can be moved as a unit or completed, so you are constantly stacking off-suit cards you cannot easily untangle. One-suit Spider is the easiest, and two-suit sits comfortably in between.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which version should a beginner start with?',
    a: 'Start with one suit. Because every card is the same suit, any descending sequence is automatically a movable, completable run, so you can learn the flow of the game without fighting the cards. Once that feels easy, move up to two suits.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the goal change with more suits?',
    a: 'No — only the difficulty changes. In every version the goal is identical: build a full descending King-to-Ace run in a single suit, which is then removed from the table. Clear all eight runs and you win. More suits simply make those same-suit runs harder to assemble.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Spider Solitaire: 1, 2, or 4 Suits? Which to Play',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', '@id': 'https://cardhearth.com/#jared', name: 'Jared Waterworth', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/spider-1-2-4-suits/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og/spider.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/spider/', label: 'Play Spider Solitaire' }}
>
  <p>
    Spider Solitaire greets you with a choice that trips up a lot of newcomers: do you want to play
    with <strong>one suit, two suits, or four suits</strong>? It sounds like three different games,
    but it isn't. The rules are identical in every version — the suit count is simply the
    <strong>difficulty dial</strong>. Turn it up and the same game gets dramatically harder. This
    guide explains what changes (and what doesn't), then helps you decide which version to
    <a href="/spider/">play</a> today.
  </p>

  <h2>The one rule that never changes</h2>
  <p>
    Spider is dealt from <strong>two full decks</strong> — 104 cards. You arrange cards into
    descending sequences in the ten tableau columns, and your goal is always the same: build a
    complete <strong>King-to-Ace run in a single suit</strong>. When you do, that run is removed
    from the table. Assemble all eight same-suit runs and you've won. That goal is fixed no matter
    how many suits are in play. What the suit count changes is how hard those same-suit runs are to
    put together — because only a genuine same-suit sequence can be picked up and moved as one
    unit, or completed and cleared.
  </p>

  <h2>One suit: the gentlest version</h2>
  <p>
    In one-suit Spider every card is a spade. That single fact transforms the game. Because there's
    only one suit, <strong>any</strong> descending sequence is automatically a same-suit run — which
    means it's always movable as a unit and always eligible to be completed and removed. You never
    have to worry about colours or matching suits; if the numbers go down in order, the cards move
    together. The result is the <strong>highest win rate</strong> of the three and the cleanest way
    to learn how Spider flows: dealing rows, opening columns, and clearing runs.
  </p>

  <h2>Two suits: the popular middle ground</h2>
  <p>
    Two-suit Spider — usually spades and hearts — is where most regular players settle. Now you have
    to keep <strong>two suits untangled</strong>. You can still stack a card on any card one rank
    higher regardless of suit, but only a same-suit sequence moves as a block, so building
    <strong>in-suit</strong> genuinely matters. Mix the suits carelessly and you'll bury cards you
    needed; plan ahead and there's real room to recover. It's a meaningful step up from one suit
    without being punishing, which is exactly why it's the default challenge for so many people.
    CardHearth has a dedicated <a href="/spider-2-suits/">2-suit Spider</a> page if you want to jump
    straight in.
  </p>

  <h2>Four suits: the full challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Four-suit Spider uses all four suits across both decks, and it is a different beast. Here, only
    a <strong>true same-suit run</strong> can be moved as a unit or completed — so off-suit stacking
    is constant and unavoidable. You'll spend much of the game parking cards on near-matches just to
    free up space, then patiently re-sorting them by suit. Win rates are low even for strong
    players, and a single misordered card can lock up a column for good. It's the version
    experienced players reach for when they want the game to fight back. Our
    <a href="/spider-4-suits/">4-suit Spider</a> page has it ready when you are.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <p>
    Think of it as a progression rather than a one-time pick:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One suit → Easy → New players.</strong> Learn the rhythm of the game with the cards
      working with you, not against you.</li>
    <li><strong>Two suits → Medium → Most players.</strong> The natural default once one suit feels
      automatic — challenging but recoverable.</li>
    <li><strong>Four suits → Hard → Experienced players.</strong> The full test, best attempted once
      two-suit games feel comfortable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In short: start at <strong>one suit</strong> to learn the flow, make <strong>two suits</strong>
    your everyday challenge, and try <strong>four suits</strong> once two-suit deals stop giving you
    trouble. CardHearth offers the two-suit and four-suit variants on their own pages, and every
    deal comes with <strong>unlimited undo and hints</strong> — the best way to practise a harder
    suit count without grinding through losses.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Ready to deal a hand? <a href="/spider/">Play Spider Solitaire</a> now, step up to
    <a href="/spider-2-suits/">2-suit Spider</a> or <a href="/spider-4-suits/">4-suit Spider</a>, or
    sharpen your play with our <a href="/guides/spider-solitaire-strategy/">Spider Solitaire
    strategy</a> guide. The <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a> covers the rest of the CardHearth
    library.
  </p>
</Article>
